's neck  compares to that of a giraffe he writes  as dismissively as one can in an  academic paper that a giraffe  has a neck that is scarcely as long as  its body  by contrast the lacrimaria's neck can  reach anywhere  from seven to eight times its body  length  as mast goes on to say if a giraffe  could pull off such a feat  then we would quote find the giraffe  browsing leaves from the tops of trees  well towards 100 feet in height  plus the lacrimaria's neck is just  so flexible able to wind itself around  debris in a way a giraffe definitely  could not but where giraffes use their  relatively shorter  and stiffer necks to forage through tree  tops lacrimaria  are much less vegan using their neck  to ambush prey alacrimaria's hunting  style  is incredibly successful  but it's built in part on  chaos instead of lying in wait for food  to come by  the lacrimaria plays the odds it  zaps its neck out incredibly fast  sub-second  movements to whip around and randomly  sample the area  around it for food this strategy  helps the lacrimaria capture all sorts  of prey  and that's why when you're looking down  at your slides you might see the damaged  remains of the lacrimaria's unfortunate  victims  before you see the hunter itself  if we saw this kind of carnage on the  savannas of east africa  pieces of animals strewn across the  landscape  none of us would ever get within a mile  of a giraffe  ever again when the lacrimaria finds its  target  it fires off a set of organelles called  extrusomes  to release toxins into their prey and  you can see that the flexibility of the  lacrimaria's neck comes in handy  not just for hunting but also for eating  expanding as it does here to contain  the entire meal  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  and even if the prey is larger or faster  than the lacrimaria and it can't get the  whole microbe  it can still tear off quite a sizable  chunk  and make a nice meal out of that piece  of course all that movement is a lot  and it's not something that the organism  can be doing all the time  our master of microscopes was excited to  find some of these lacrimaria so he  could observe them in action  only to find that they'd become much  less active their necks would pull  further and further inward until it was  retracted  entirely and then the ciliate itself was  completely still  lacrimaria are rare enough to find as it  is  and when james saw this strange  stillness  he was worried that he might be doing  something that was hurting his  precious friends months later he found  some more lacrimaria alor  but after 10 minutes of watching them  they did this same weird  dormant pose thing again concerned again  that he might be killing them james  left the slide in a humidity chamber  overnight so  imagine his relief when the next morning  he found that the slide was  full of reproducing lacrimaria  that's when james noticed that this  activity and dormancy  seemed to be a repeated cycle the  lacrimaria  would awake and be active for about 10  minutes  and then retreat back to their inactive  state for another 10 minutes and then  become active again scientists studying  this phenomenon have used  high-speed cameras to find that  lacrimaria cycle  between four total states there's an  active state where the microbes neck is  out and about to find some food then  there's a resting state where it all  retracts  and then there's an activation and an  inactivation state that transitions the  microbe between  active and resting possibly allowing for  the structures  underlying the neck to rearrange as the  lacrimaria moves from  one way of life to the next and so while  it's resting the lacrimaria is much less  of a threat  its neck which again is not actually a  neck retracts  leaving its neighbors in a moment of  peace but  when it awakens it will return to  twisting  and turning and elongating menacing the  microcosmos  until it has made a meal of it  thank you for coming on this journey  with us as we explore  the unseen world that surrounds us  if you are a us citizen and you want to  not only keep imagining the world  complexly but also  have a voice in your government check  out how to vote in every state  the rules for voting are different  depending on where you live  so we've made a whole series of videos  explaining how to get your vote on  in each and every state as well as some  advice for special cases like  territories and overseas  voters this year we also put together a  guide for first-time  voters with help from our friends at  mediawise  to help you navigate everything from  your news diet  to political ads to money in politics  you can find all that  over at youtube.com how to vote in every  state  thank you as always to all of the people  on the screen right now  they are our patrons on patreon we have  two  patreon tiers now a two dollar tier and  an eight dollar tier so go on over  to patreon.com journey to micro if you  want to be one of the people  who make it possible for this show to  exist at all  if you want to see more from our master  of microscopes james check out  jam and germs on instagram and if you  want to see more from us  there is always a subscribe button  somewhere nearby
 

